## Conversation
Found while bringing up Kimi-K3: the image was built with a patched FlashInfer
but ran stock, so the SiTU code path did not exist at runtime.

## Actions
Added --force-reinstall --no-deps to the flashinfer wheel install and a
non-fatal `pip check`. Verified in-cluster before and after: a pre-fix image
reported `hasattr(ActivationType, "Situ") == False` with
flashinfer-python 0.6.15.post1 from PyPI; the post-fix image reports True.

## Errors & Mistakes
Cost two full image builds (r4, r5) before being noticed. The tell was
`AttributeError: type object 'ActivationType' has no attribute 'Situ'` at
serving time from an image whose build log showed the patch applying cleanly --
if a patch is applied at build time but absent at runtime, suspect the install
order before re-reading the patch.

## Dead Ends
Bumping the local wheel version to dodge "already satisfied" -- unnecessary
once --force-reinstall is used, and it would have leaked a fake version string
into the image.

## Hints for Future Agents
`-c /tmp/constraints.txt` was dropped from this install deliberately: with
--no-deps there is nothing left to constrain, and the constraints file covers
torch/vision/audio/xformers only. Validated by the images built from this tree.

## Codebase Discoveries
The vLLM wheel's own requirements are what introduce flashinfer-python; nothing
in this Dockerfile asks for it directly, which is why the shadowing was easy to
miss.

## Open Questions
Whether any other locally built wheel in this Dockerfile is shadowed the same
way -- lmcache, infinistore, deepep and nixl all install after vLLM.
